Q: How to modify the fill color of an SVG image when being served as background image? A: <p>I use this work around:</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>section {
    position: relative;
}

section::before {
    content: &quot;&quot;;
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: -1;
    background: var(--bg-some-color);

    -webkit-mask-image: url(some.svg);
    -webkit-mask-repeat: no-repeat;
    -webkit-mask-position: center;
    -webkit-mask-size: cover;

    mask-image: url(some.svg);
    mask-repeat: no-repeat;
    mask-position: center;
    mask-size: cover;
}
</code></pre>

Q: FLTK/Wayland/X11 custom drawing in a pop-up window A: <p>Disclaimer: I have never used FLTK nor Yoshimi before, so I do not know what I am doing on that front.</p>
<p>Disclaimer: The current state of this question does not really feel suitable for StackOverflow to me (the reproducer is way too large, see <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/help/minimal-reproducible-example">https://stackoverflow.com/help/minimal-reproducible-example</a> and this is now a &quot;please help debugging this&quot; question). I will go with it anyway.</p>
<p>What I did so far: I got the reproducing program in version 48c00ba73b311effa530c7372c89102fb70a18a6 and tried to find the right pieces of code. I am not sure whether I ended up looking at the correct code.</p>
<p>What I did find: Your program is drawing the red lines to the wrong window. And in a way so that they seem to stay after drawing. I figured that out by asking cairo do write out a PNG file of what was being drawn:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>diff --git a/src/UI/WidgetPDial.cpp b/src/UI/WidgetPDial.cpp
index 254ffb21..3bcdb07b 100644
--- a/src/UI/WidgetPDial.cpp
+++ b/src/UI/WidgetPDial.cpp
@@ -301,6 +301,10 @@ void WidgetPDial::draw()
     cairo_surface_destroy(Xsurface);
     cairo_destroy(cr);
 #endif
+
+    static int counter = 0;
+    std::string fileName = &quot;/tmp/img/image&quot; + std::to_string(counter++) + &quot;.png&quot;;
+    cairo_surface_write_to_png(cairo_get_target(cr), fileName.c_str());
 }

 inline void WidgetPDial::pdialcolor(int r,int g,int b)
</code></pre>
<p>The result is not a screenshot of the popup, but of the main UI and the red lines are indeed visible:
<a href="https://i.sstatic.net/iVHZuWnj.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/iVHZuWnj.png" alt="Output produced via the above patch" /></a></p>
<p>I get similar results with the following:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>diff --git a/src/UI/MiscGui.cpp b/src/UI/MiscGui.cpp
index f5f2fabc..57b0cac1 100644
--- a/src/UI/MiscGui.cpp
+++ b/src/UI/MiscGui.cpp
@@ -859,6 +859,9 @@ void custom_graphics(ValueType vt, float val,int W,int H)
             cairo_line_to(cr, x0 + _w, cy - ry * log10(powf(0.05, p)));
             cairo_stroke(cr);
             cairo_restore(cr);
+    static int counter = 0;
+    std::string fileName = &quot;/tmp/img/2image&quot; + std::to_string(counter++) + &quot;.png&quot;;
+    cairo_surface_write_to_png(cairo_get_target(cr), fileName.c_str());
 #ifndef YOSHIMI_CAIRO_LEGACY
             Fl::cairo_flush(cr);
 #else
</code></pre>
<p>As written above, I have no clue about FLTK. So I looked around and found <a href="https://github.com/fltk/fltk/blob/master/README.Cairo.txt" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://github.com/fltk/fltk/blob/master/README.Cairo.txt</a>. What I found there does not fit into what I find in your code. You seem to be using cairo to add things to the &quot;stuff&quot; that FLTK draws? I am not sure whether that is supposed to work with FLTK. But something somewhere ends up going to the wrong window. Random guess would be: <code>DynTooltip</code> is a <code> Fl_Menu_Window</code></p>
<p>I <em>think</em> the popup comes from <code>src/UI/DynamicTooltip.cpp</code>, but I got lost in that code. So instead, since the question is about drawing a red line in the popup, the following works fine for me (but obviously that is not the line where you want it to be - it is just a proof of concept):</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>diff --git a/src/UI/DynamicTooltip.cpp b/src/UI/DynamicTooltip.cpp
index 12d02e26..e55ccbb0 100644
--- a/src/UI/DynamicTooltip.cpp
+++ b/src/UI/DynamicTooltip.cpp
@@ -289,6 +289,10 @@ void DynTooltip::draw()
     /* Draw additional graphics */
     if (graphicsType != VC_plainValue)
         custom_graphics(graphicsType, currentValue, w(), h() - mh);
+
+    // Since the question is about a red line, I am just trying to produce a red line
+    fl_color(255, 0, 0);
+    fl_line(10, 10, 50, 50);
 }

 /*
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/BOIgYqHz.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/BOIgYqHz.png" alt="Adding a red line at a random position" /></a></p>
<p>I do not feel like this is a good answer, but perhaps it is a good start for a discussion? If so, I would propose that we discuss this elsewhere, e.g. in an issue of the Yoshimi project (feel free to <code>@psychon</code> me if you want to create one).</p>
<p>Edit: Or does the following help you a bit? Why don't you use FLTK to draw the line (despite &quot;line width of 1.5 not possible&quot;)?</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>diff --git a/src/UI/MiscGui.cpp b/src/UI/MiscGui.cpp
index f5f2fabc..4a38bf56 100644
--- a/src/UI/MiscGui.cpp
+++ b/src/UI/MiscGui.cpp
@@ -852,13 +852,9 @@ void custom_graphics(ValueType vt, float val,int W,int H)
                  Fl_Window::current()-&gt;w(), Fl_Window::current()-&gt;h());
             cairo_t *cr = cairo_create (Xsurface);
 #endif
-            cairo_save(cr);
-            cairo_set_source_rgb(cr, 1, 0, 0);
-            cairo_set_line_width(cr, 1.5);
-            cairo_move_to(cr, x0, cy - ry * log10(power&lt;50&gt;(p)));
-            cairo_line_to(cr, x0 + _w, cy - ry * log10(powf(0.05, p)));
-            cairo_stroke(cr);
-            cairo_restore(cr);
+            fl_color(255, 0, 0);
+            fl_line_style(FL_SOLID, 2, NULL);
+            fl_line(x0, cy - ry * log10(power&lt;50&gt;(p)), x0 + _w, cy - ry * log10(powf(0.05, p)));
 #ifndef YOSHIMI_CAIRO_LEGACY
             Fl::cairo_flush(cr);
 #else
</code></pre>
